- I wanna talk about how I
wanna be reincarnated as a dog.

00:08:57.243 --> 00:08:59.370
- It's okay to stop caring, you know,

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:02.070
there's just too much,
there's way too much.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:05.670
- 'Cause right now we're just
so overloaded with information

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:07.710
and things to care about.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.330
- I literally gotta shit
myself if somebody sneeze

00:09:10.330 --> 00:09:11.367
within two feet of me,

00:09:11.367 --> 00:09:14.300
and now I gotta worry
about what's going on?

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.160
You know, in multiple countries now.

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:18.380
I'm a UK, I live in Japan,

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:20.041
gotta keep up with dual politics?

00:09:20.041 --> 00:09:21.610
- That's the biggest thing
that's changed, right?

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:24.270
Because before, everyone
only needed to care

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:25.750
about what was around them.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:27.220
And now being people on the internet,

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:30.620
I have to care about
everything going on globally.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:32.100
And it gets to the point

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:34.080
where there are so many
different cultures,

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:36.410
so many different points of view,

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:38.060
so many different like experiences

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:39.750
that each individual has had,

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:42.620
that sometimes it's
impossible for a lot of people

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:46.040
to get the same viewpoint on things.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:48.890
Because even for us who've
lived in multiple countries

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:52.480
and have had experience
with multiple cultures.

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.568
You can say we're cultured ourselves.

00:09:54.568 --> 00:09:57.400
(laughs)

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:00.710
Saying this in like the
pure, unironic sense,

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:03.420
because you get this
sense whenever I go home,

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.350
or whenever I hear about
any of us going home

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.230
and you see people who have
stayed in their home towns

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:10.750
and you see people who have
not left their home towns

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:12.470
and they have the exact same viewpoint

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:14.102
that they got raised on, right?

00:10:14.102 --> 00:10:17.010
And coming from the place,

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:21.350
you kind of understand how
they got that viewpoint

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:22.700
even though you don't agree with it.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:23.533
You know what I mean?

00:10:23.533 --> 00:10:26.596
But you also understand that
they don't have anything

00:10:26.596 --> 00:10:28.830
to really change their viewpoint in there

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:30.270
because they've never
experienced anything else.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:32.010
- There's no new information that comes in

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:34.700
to stimulate that change
in thought process.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:36.830
So forever they just think,
well this is correct,

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:40.040
because this is what
it was like around me.

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:41.020
I don't know about anything else

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:41.860
that's happening in the world,

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:44.240
so this is just how I'm
going to see the world.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:49.000
But then, for instance, I
remember when I first came back

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.660
to Australia after I was living here

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.820
and I would go see my
friends and stuff like that

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.520
who have been there the entire time.

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:59.450
I dunno, things that I
remember I used to be able

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:03.040
to very heavily relate with
and kind of almost agree upon,

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:06.220
I found myself to be like,
oh, is this how I felt?

00:11:07.176 --> 00:11:08.980
Is this really what my
thought process was like?

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.900
And it's so weird because
like that didn't even happen

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.160
in that much of a large space of time.

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:16.795
Like that only maybe happened
in two years or something,

00:11:16.795 --> 00:11:18.210
of just living here.

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:21.260
And I think definitely doing
YouTube and stuff like that

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:22.490
has got me to interact

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:26.427
with so many more different
nationalities and cultures

00:11:26.427 --> 00:11:28.160
and stuff like that.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:31.180
Doing this job, you're almost
forced to be up-to-date

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.593
with everything that's
happening in the world

00:11:32.593 --> 00:11:34.510
because you have people all over the world

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:35.610
who are viewing you, right?

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:38.500
So I have this weird pressure of being,

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:40.330
I don't want this one
person from this one country

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:41.350
coming into my streams
or whatever and being,

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:43.537
do you know what's happening
in my country right now?

00:11:43.537 --> 00:11:45.840
And me going, no, I don't.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:46.690
- Yeah, you get that,
like what do you think

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:49.870
about the current gentrification
of Southeast Macau,

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:51.290
and you're like, what the fuck?

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:52.417
How the fuck would I know that.

00:11:52.417 --> 00:11:53.397
- Wat is Macau?

00:11:53.397 --> 00:11:55.480
(laughs)

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:56.740
- You get that a lot.

00:11:56.740 --> 00:11:59.180
You surely can't expect me
to have a horse in this race,

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:00.013
please, come on.

00:12:00.013 --> 00:12:03.026
- I'm just kidding people from
Macau, I know what Macau is.

00:12:03.026 --> 00:12:06.040
But that's the thing,
you're almost pressured

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:07.223
to be up to date on that kind of stuff,

00:12:07.223 --> 00:12:09.410
because you don't wanna
leave any of your viewers out

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:10.243
and stuff like that.

00:12:10.243 --> 00:12:13.110
And so, that causes you to just,

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:15.390
it absorbs so much
information about the world

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:18.560
that when I go back to my
hometown and I meet people

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:20.550
who aren't up to date
on that kind of stuff

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.350
who have completely different
viewpoints of the world,

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:23.935
because the amount of information

00:12:23.935 --> 00:12:26.838
that they absorb about the
world is on a different level.

00:12:26.838 --> 00:12:29.816
It's, I dunno, it's a bit of a whiplash.

00:12:29.816 --> 00:12:31.730
I'd be like, ooh, I can't believe
I used to think like that.

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:32.563
That's kinda weird.

00:12:32.563 --> 00:12:33.770
- I don't even know my I'm blood type,

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:36.880
so I don't even expect people
to learn about the outside.

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:38.660
How do people expect me
to keep up with weird news

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:39.493
in some country I've never heard of.

00:12:39.493 --> 00:12:41.510
I think you should be the one
to know your own blood type.

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:44.177
- I'm sure I should, I have
a blood disease as well.

00:12:44.177 --> 00:12:45.340
(laughs)

00:12:45.340 --> 00:12:46.173
- I have a blood disease,

00:12:46.173 --> 00:12:48.670
I don't know what my blood type is though.

00:12:48.670 --> 00:12:51.640
- I don't know.
- It's pretty irresponsible.

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:54.290
You also had to do a blood test here,

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:55.840
and you were shown your blood type.

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:57.170
Having said that, I don't
know my blood type either,

00:12:57.170 --> 00:12:58.750
I got the same...
- What!

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:02.190
- I'm just as monkey-brained
as him apparently.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:03.480
- I know it's in the alphabet.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:04.870
Like it's one of them.

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:06.050
- It's in the alphabet!

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:07.513
- It's one of the four.

00:13:08.625 --> 00:13:09.458
- What is your blood type?

00:13:09.458 --> 00:13:10.416
Sigma.

00:13:10.416 --> 00:13:12.070
(laughs)

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:13.810
- Delta, alpha, sigma.

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:14.643
- Alpha, sigma.

00:13:14.643 --> 00:13:15.500
- I'm like, decaf?

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:16.392
I don't know.

00:13:16.392 --> 00:13:18.824
(laughs)

00:13:18.824 --> 00:13:21.010
I don't know, and I do, like you said,

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:22.270
you do try to educate
yourself on some things,

00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:23.710
but there's just so, you know,

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:26.100
you can't be expected to
keep up with so many things.

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:27.950
- Like here's a question then,

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:30.180
do you feel like there's a big difference

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:34.790
between explaining to
someone your perspective

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:38.140
and actually having the
person really experience it

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:38.973
and empathize with you?

00:13:38.973 --> 00:13:40.440
'Cause I feel like those
are two different things.

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:44.550
- Yeah, I can sit here,
I could go back to Wales

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:47.590
and explain Japanese culture all day

00:13:47.590 --> 00:13:48.790
and they just won't understand it.

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:50.150
- Yeah, exactly.

00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:51.320
- Why would you do that?

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:53.330
Why would you bow at
every single, every word?

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:56.017
Why would you say, excuse
me, every 10 seconds.

00:13:56.017 --> 00:13:58.180
- To me, it's kind of very similar to,

00:13:58.180 --> 00:13:59.320
let's say learning a language,

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:00.840
where you can explain a language,

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:03.738
you can explain grammar
structures and you can explain

00:14:03.738 --> 00:14:05.730
how the writing system and stuff works.

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:08.570
But there's a difference
between understanding

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:12.210
how a language works and
actually just absorbing it

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:14.913
and really getting a feel
for the language yourself.

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:17.970
I know there's a word for it,

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:19.544
acquisition, language acquisition,

00:14:19.544 --> 00:14:23.850
versus just learning how it
works on a logical sense.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:25.920
And I feel the exact same way

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:28.700
when it comes to cultures and perspective

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:30.420
where there's been so many times

00:14:30.420 --> 00:14:33.340
where someone has told
me about a perspective

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:35.490
and you're logically like,
"Yeah, I agree with that".

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:37.530
You know, they could be
making the most sense,

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:39.720
but then you kind of
maybe move to the country

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:41.900
that they're living in
or you go travel there

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:44.414
or you meet more people
from that same culture.

00:14:44.414 --> 00:14:46.960
And you're like, oh,
I think I actually get

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:49.020
what you're talking about now, you know?

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:51.730
- Yeah, I think it's like
how when you're growing up

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:53.280
and you hear about other
countries and you think,

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.500
oh, it's that funny little
thing that country does.

00:14:56.500 --> 00:14:59.100
I know they would probably
do this about Japan a lot,

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:00.450
they would just show
with things like Sumo,

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:02.759
and they'd be like, isn't that so strange.

00:15:02.759 --> 00:15:05.990
- These big men in diapers
pushing each other around.

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:07.210
- It's one of those things, aint it?

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:10.640
But I think that when you get
to experience more cultures,

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:13.927
your mindset on when you see
stuff like that changes a lot.

00:15:13.927 --> 00:15:15.890
You go from thinking,
oh, that funny thing,

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:18.350
that weird, strange thing, to thinking

00:15:18.350 --> 00:15:19.760
I'm sure there's a story behind it.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:21.330
I'm sure there's a history,
there's a culture behind it.

00:15:21.330 --> 00:15:22.460
- I definitely felt that,

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:24.440
especially after I started dating Aki

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:27.120
and you know, through YouTube
meeting a lot of Americans,

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.460
because in Australia, the image of America

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:32.820
is not that glamorous
or positive in any way.

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:34.470
I'm just saying it's straight up.

00:15:35.582 --> 00:15:38.700
- I don't think the image
of Americans in America.

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:40.910
I love that the Americans
are often the first people

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:42.090
to bash their own country.
- Right, exactly.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.930
- I love you, America.

00:15:43.930 --> 00:15:44.820
- It depends on where they're from.

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:45.653
- Yeah, it depends.

00:15:45.653 --> 00:15:47.510
But after actually going to America

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:49.010
and talking to Americans
and stuff like that,

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:51.370
that completely flipped it
over and I realized, oh,

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:54.500
the problem was just
kind of my environment

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:57.420
that was pushing that agenda
and the media that I consumed

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:59.930
that was kind of pushing
this sort of negative agenda

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:01.667
about American culture
and stuff like that.

00:16:01.667 --> 00:16:03.900
But then when you actually
go and you live it out,

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:05.619
you see it firsthand and
you see everything else

00:16:05.619 --> 00:16:06.640
that they don't show and you're like,

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:09.470
oh, actually it's not as insane or wacky

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:12.365
as the TV shows or the shit
my friends were talking about,

00:16:12.365 --> 00:16:13.270
make it out to be, you know?

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:17.240
- Yeah, but I remember when I
first started dating Sydney.

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:21.270
I remember when we first met
and first started dating,

00:16:21.270 --> 00:16:22.230
I remember early on,

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:24.230
'cause she mentioned
this to me the other day.

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:27.240
I asked her, so out of curiosity,

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:28.737
what do you actually think about America?

00:16:28.737 --> 00:16:30.690
And she was like, oh,
greatest country in the world.

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:32.200
And I remember.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:35.260
- Was she dead-ass when she said it?

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:37.810
- Imagine, she came from a
small town in the Midwest

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:38.977
- Oh when you first started dating?

00:16:38.977 --> 00:16:43.600
- Yeah, when I first started
dating her five, six years ago,

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:45.350
shit, seven years ago now actually.

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:47.920
It was a long time ago and I remember

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:50.670
that was the first time
she had basically traveled

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:53.020
out of America, and she
came from a very small town

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:56.280
in the Midwest and got
raised with the values

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:59.060
that you would expect a
small town to get raised in.

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:00.670
I remember she said, oh yeah,

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:01.897
America is the greatest country
in the world, obviously.

00:17:01.897 --> 00:17:03.740
And I remember, apparently she said,

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:04.856
I was just like, "Hmm".

00:17:04.856 --> 00:17:06.260
(laughs)

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:07.093
Like that.

00:17:10.827 --> 00:17:13.010
- And she said to me at the time,

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:15.743
she got like genuinely offended, right?

00:17:17.482 --> 00:17:20.031
She straight up, she got
genuinely angry at me

00:17:20.031 --> 00:17:20.864
at the time for doing that.

00:17:20.864 --> 00:17:23.440
She was like, what'd you mean, why?

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:24.830
Why did you make that sound?

00:17:24.830 --> 00:17:26.320
She said to me, and I was like,

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:28.613
so what constitutes the greatest country?

00:17:28.613 --> 00:17:30.880
And why is America the greatest country?

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:32.960
And she was like, oh, land
of the free, isn't it?

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:33.931
We got freedom.

00:17:33.931 --> 00:17:34.764
- I was about to say.

00:17:34.764 --> 00:17:36.167
And second, I'm like, "Hmm".

00:17:37.962 --> 00:17:39.110
- I was about to say,

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:40.003
if she's about to drop the freedom bomb,

00:17:40.003 --> 00:17:43.020
I don't know what I can say to that.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:44.210
- No, because to me at the time,

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:46.160
it was just living up to

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.430
the stereotypical patriotic American,

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:52.380
'cause America is a
good country, you know?

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:55.750
But my issue is, the more I
travel, the more I'm just like,

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:57.057
is there a greatest country in the world?

00:17:57.057 --> 00:17:58.350
And there's no way you can define

00:17:58.350 --> 00:17:59.331
the greatest country in the world.

00:17:59.331 --> 00:18:00.760
- Otherwise everyone will be living there.

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:01.593
- Yeah, exactly.

00:18:01.593 --> 00:18:03.740
And to me, patriotism,
as a British person,

00:18:03.740 --> 00:18:05.550
is just so weird.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:09.880
Like, you see it, I see
American patriotism where,

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:12.250
you know, not saying
every American like this,

00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:15.410
but especially some of
the Americans I've met

00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:17.250
who were raised in small towns,

00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:20.550
especially when I go back
to Sydney's hometown,

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:25.550
it's just, you see this
culture that's been,

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:29.210
and this idea that's just been propagated

00:18:29.210 --> 00:18:30.530
through the small town,

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:33.390
and there's not really
been any other perspective

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:34.883
to really change that perspective.

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:41.800
They probably believe, by their belief,

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:43.410
they probably believe that America

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:45.850
is the greatest country in the world

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:49.630
and there is no other thing
that can challenge that.

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:52.881
And it's just, I can't
blame them for that.

00:18:52.881 --> 00:18:54.370
- No of course not.
- And I'm not even saying

00:18:54.370 --> 00:18:56.100
America isn't the greatest
country in the world.

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:58.990
I'm just saying that because their belief

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:01.360
has been permeated so deeply
that there's been nothing

00:19:01.360 --> 00:19:02.930
to really break that mold for them.

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:04.790
And it took years for Sydney

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:06.280
to really kinda change her perspective.

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:08.143
It didn't take like just
one trip to England.

00:19:08.143 --> 00:19:12.600
It took years for her traveling
to living with me in England

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:15.970
and living with me in Thailand
